// Cascade: Alastair Gowans' shrimp fly, orange and yellow cascading over a silver and black body.

id cascade
name "Cascade"
type salmon
imitates attractor
difficulty intermediate
species salmon sea-trout
// Assumption: the months of a Scottish salmon season; Gowans fishes it "at any time of year".
season february march april may june july august september october
origin originator="Alastair Gowans" region="Scotland"
description "An oval silver tag, a long tail of hot orange and yellow bucktail mixed with clear flash, a body of flat silver tinsel behind and black floss in front ribbed with oval silver, a black squirrel wing with silver flash, and long yellow and hot orange collar hackles."
history """
    Alastair "Ally" Gowans, who tied Ally's Shrimp, set out to make a pretty fly from Krystal
    Hair, a big ball of the silver kind given him by a well-known French angler and a hank of the
    clear. He named it after the way the two colours of the tail cascade over the hook. The first
    fish came the April after he tied it, on the Tummel below the road bridge at Pitlochry: a
    springer of about 14 lb, beached by a friend. His first flies had a black hair wing on top and
    underneath; on his first trebles he left the underwing out, and later versions drop it.
    Gowans also tied the first variant, with gold for the silver half of the body. John Gray
    reckons that within little more than a decade it became probably the most used salmon fly in
    Britain.
    """
fishing """
    Gowans fishes it in almost any conditions and at any time of year: tiny doubles and trebles
    of #12 and smaller in the lowest summer water, hooks up to #4 when the water is higher or
    colder, and tubes in high, cold water early in the spring and late in the autumn. Håkan
    Karsnäser finds it at its best in clear water and bright sunshine.
    """
sources {
    "Let's Fly Fish: The Cascade salmon fly (Alastair Gowans)" url="https://letsflyfish.com/cascade_salmon_fly.htm"
    "Trout & Salmon Fishing: Cascade step by step (John Gray)" url="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/cascade-step-by-step.htm"
    "Trout & Salmon Fishing: Cascade tube flies (John Gray)" url="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/cascade-tube-flies.htm"
    "Ahrex: The evolution of salmon flies, appearance and styling (Håkan Karsnäser)" url="https://ahrexhooks.com/2026/02/06/the-evolution-of-salmon-flies-appearance-and-styling/"
    "Fish4Flies: Cascade, Ken Sawada double" url="https://www.fish4flies.com/Salmon-Atlantic/Ken-Sawada-Doubles/Cascade"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Cascade (video, Casper Hansen)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/video/cascade-1"
    "Flugor.se: Cascade Shrimp" url="https://www.flugor.se/flugor/laxflugor-dubbel/cascade-shrimp-1754"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/cascade-sbs-5s.jpg" page="https://www.trout-salmon-fishing.com/cascade-step-by-step.htm" author="John Gray" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Single hook, side view"
    "https://ahrexhooks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Cascade-by-Hakan-Karsnaser-12-1024x661.jpg" page="https://ahrexhooks.com/2026/02/06/the-evolution-of-salmon-flies-appearance-and-styling/" author="Håkan Karsnäser" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Double in the vise"
    "https://www.fish4flies.com/img/flies/Large/3245-3245%20Cascade.jpg" page="https://www.fish4flies.com/Salmon-Atlantic/Ken-Sawada-Doubles/Cascade" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Double, long wing and flash"
}

// Assumption: a double, as most of the photos and shops show; Gowans ties it on singles,
// doubles, trebles and tubes alike.
hook salmon-double size=8
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    tag oval-tinsel color=silver
    // Read from the photos: a sparse, wispy tail.
    tail bucktail color="#f2581a" mix=yellow count=70 label="Mixed hot orange and yellow bucktail"
    tail-flash krystal-flash color=clear count=6 label="Clear Krystal Hair"
    rib oval-tinsel color=silver
    rear flat-tinsel color=silver
    front silk-floss color=black
    wing squirrel-tail color=black
    flash krystal-flash color=silver count=6 label="Silver Krystal Hair"
    collar wet-fly-cock-hackle color=yellow fibre=2.2 sweep=58
    front-collar wet-fly-cock-hackle color="#f2581a" fibre=2.1 sweep=58 label="Hot orange cock hackle"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.86
    wrap-thread to=0.0 note="A bed of thread to above the hook point."
    tie-in tag
    wrap-thread to=0.06
    wind tag to=0.06 turns=5 note="Five or six close turns."
    tie-off tag
    // Karsnäser: one to one and a half shanks long; Gray's photo about a shank and a third.
    tie-in tail length=3.4 angle=4 spread=14 note="Mix the two colours well, and bind the butts along the shank."
    trim tail
    // Gray's photo: the flash a little longer than the hair.
    tie-in tail-flash length=3.7 angle=5 spread=12 note="A few strands folded round the thread, on top of the tail."
    tie-in rib
    tie-in rear
    wrap-thread to=0.48
    wind rear to=0.48 turns=10 note="To the tail and back to just past the middle, in touching turns."
    tie-off rear
    tie-in front
    wrap-thread to=0.82
    wind front to=0.82 turns=8
    tie-off front
    wind rib to=0.82 turns=5 note="Four or five even turns over both halves."
    tie-off rib
    // Karsnäser: the wing as long as the shank; Gray's photo a little longer.
    tie-in wing length=2.8 angle=7 spread=16 note="A small bunch, low over the body."
    trim wing
    tie-in flash length=3.0 angle=8 spread=12 note="A few strands over the wing."
    tie-in collar
    wrap-thread to=0.86
    // Karsnäser: the fibres reach the bend.
    wind collar to=0.86 turns=3 note="Two or three turns, stroking the fibres back."
    tie-off collar
    tie-in front-collar
    wrap-thread to=0.9
    wind front-collar to=0.9 turns=3 note="In front of the yellow, the fibres about as long."
    tie-off front-collar
    whip-finish note="Two or three coats of varnish."
}
